-- title:
--   Ordinary corner: reduction to identity iff inertia acts cyclotomically
-- statement:
--   Setting. Let $p$ be an odd prime ($hp2 : p \neq 2$), let $M$ be a nonzero natural number with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and let $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ be a subgroup containing every unit whose image under the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times} \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^{\times}$ is trivial (`hHp`). Write $J_H(M)$ for [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127), the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot F_H(M)$ (`xHFunctionFieldBar M H`) over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and $T_p J_H(M)$ for the Tate module [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in $J_H(M)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$. A set of primes $S$ and the hypothesis `hin`, asserting the Hecke and diamond inputs `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H` at level $\Gamma_H(M)$, are fixed.
--
--   Hecke algebra group. A commutative ring $\mathbb{T}$, a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra acting on $T_p J_H(M)$ compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action, is given together with: faithfulness of the action (`hfaith`), a map $\mathrm{op}$ from the generators [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the symbols $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, and $\langle d\rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$) to $\mathbb{T}$ whose elements act as the corresponding covariant operators `tateGenOpH` on $T_p J_H(M)$ (`hop`), and the requirement that the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of $\mathrm{op}$ be all of $\mathbb{T}$ (`hgen`). Further, $S'$ is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb{T}$ — a finite family of idempotents $e_i$ that is complete and orthogonal, together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak{m}_i$ exhausting all maximal ideals of $\mathbb{T}$ and satisfying $e_i \in \mathfrak{m}_j \iff i \neq j$ — and $i_0$ is an index with the ordinarity condition $\mathrm{op}(U_p) \notin \mathfrak{m}_{i_0}$ (`hord`).
--
--   Geometric data at $p$. Let $Pl$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ in the sense that $p$ is a non-unit of $Pl$ (`hPl`), with residue field of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed. Let $hj$ record that the $q$-expansion of $j$ lies in the function field of the full modular group, let $\mathfrak{X}$ be an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` (a two-chart integral model of the modular curve at $p$ together with a curve model $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` and the comparison data), let $\Lambda$ be a `LevelData p M H hpM Pl` at level $M/p$, let `hΛ` be the `AbelianSchemePropertyBundle` for $\Lambda.f$ (smooth, proper, connected fibres, carrying a relative group law), and let $O$ be a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`: a scheme $O.G$ over $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the localisation `baseRing p` with relative group law $O.L$, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M)$ onto the sections over the generic geometric point, Galois- and Hecke-equivariance, and the remaining smoothness, separatedness, fibre and torsion data of that structure. Its subgroups $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,m$ (generated by the $m$-torsion classes whose associated section extends to the place) and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,m$ are used below.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi pin. The hypotheses `hD`, `hDQ` assert that the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designation built from $(O.G, O.g)$ with zero section the identity of $O.L$ represents the fibrewise-algebraically-trivial rigidified line-bundle condition `algEquivZeroCut` over the integral model and over its base change to $\mathbb{Q}$; `hsep` asserts that the generic fibre morphism is separated. A section $ajQ$ of the base-changed designation, a morphism $kQ$ between the two pullbacks of the model, a morphism $ajbar : \mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C \to O.G$ and a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\varepsilon bar$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ over the base are given, subject to: an isomorphism of the Poincaré bundle over $\mathbb{Q}$ with the pullback of the integral Poincaré bundle (`hpoinc`); normalisation of $ajQ$ along the zero section (`hajQε`); the universal Abel–Jacobi property `hajQ`, stating that for every field $K$, every $\mathbb{Q}$-point $t$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,K$ and every $K$-point $x$ of the base-changed curve, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $ajQ$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor cut out by the section $\varepsilon$; the compatibilities `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂` of $kQ$ with the two projections (the second up to the map $\mathbb{Q} \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$); the definition `hajbar` of $ajbar$ as $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$ followed by $kQ$, by $ajQ$ and by the first projection; the statement `hajbar_over` that $ajbar$ lies over the generic point; the normalisations `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj` of $\varepsilon bar$; the additivity `hpts_law` of $O.\mathrm{pts}$ for the relative group law attached to `hD`; and `hAJ`, which states that for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ normalised as in `hεbar`, there is a degree-zero divisor equal to the difference of the places of $x$ and $s$ whose class is sent by $O.\mathrm{pts}$ to $x$ followed by $ajbar$.
--
--   Level data representability and inertia. `hrepΛ` asserts that the designation built from $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ with zero section the identity of $\Lambda.L$ represents the same condition over the model at level $\Gamma_N(p, M, H)$. `hinertF` asserts that for every $m > 0$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and every $m$-torsion class $x$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$, the difference $\sigma \cdot x - x$ lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,m$.
--
--   The inertia ring. $R$ is a commutative domain that is a henselian local ring with algebraically closed residue field, a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra with injective structure map, whose image lies in $Pl$ (`hRA`), which is a discrete valuation ring (`hRdvr`) in which $p$ is irreducible (`hRirr`), whose image is fixed exactly by the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ (`hRfix`: $\sigma$ lies in inertia iff it fixes the image of $R$ pointwise), and which is maximal with that property in the sense that every inertia-invariant element of $Pl$ comes from $R$ (`hRmax`).
--
--   The finite-part layer. $\mathcal{G}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) — a tower of finite free cocommutative Hopf $R$-algebras $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals — and $\Delta$ is an additive map from $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $J_H(M)$, $e$ a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map from $T_p$ of those points to $T_p J_H(M)$. The hypotheses are: injectivity of $\Delta$ (`hΔinj`); the level identification `hΔlev`, that $y \in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ iff $y$ is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point; Galois equivariance `hΔgal` of $\Delta$ for pairs $(\tau, \tau')$ of automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and over $R$ agreeing pointwise; Hecke compatibility `hΔhecke`, that each generator $g$ is realised by a compatible system of coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi$ of the levels inducing `genOpH` through $\Delta$; the coordinatewise description `he` of $e$ by $\Delta$, its injectivity `heinj`, the characterisation `herange` of its range as the sequences with $n$-th term in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$, Galois equivariance `hegal` with respect to $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{tateModuleRep}$ and `JH.tateGaloisRep`, saturation `hsat` ($p y$ in the range implies $y$ in the range), and `hcoker`, an isomorphism of the cokernel of $e$ with $\mathbb{Z}_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$; finally `htor`, that toric points of level $p^v$ are in the image of level-$v$ points under $\Delta$.
--
--   The toric quotient. $\mathcal{B}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p hB`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) with a compatible system $\psi$ of coalgebra maps $\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ (`hψt`), subject to $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + hB$ (`hhB`), $hB = 2h'$ (`hhB2`), the kernel description `hψker` (a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ dies under $\psi$ iff its image under $\Delta$ is toric), surjectivity `hψsurj` of the induced map on points, the reduction criterion `hψred` (if the $\psi$-image of a point reduces to the identity, measured by $Pl$-valuation of $\mathrm{toAlgHom}(x)(a) - \varepsilon(a)$ being $<1$, then so does the point itself), and the period condition `hperiod`: for $\sigma$ in inertia, $z$ a $p^v$-torsion class and $y$ a level-$v$ point with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\psi$-image of $y$ reduces to the identity.
--
--   Compatibility of the $p$-divisible group with the Néron object. A ring homomorphism $\rho h : R_p \to R$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ are given with: compatibility `hρh` of $\rho h$ with the structure maps to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hιbase`, that $\iota_v$ lies over $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\rho h$; `hιcl`, that the induced morphism to the fibre product is a closed immersion; `hιp`, that $\iota_v$ followed by multiplication by $p^v$ is the identity section; `hιpts`, that $O.\mathrm{pts}(\Delta(x))$ is the point of $\mathcal{G}$ followed by $\iota_v$; `hιmul`, additivity of $\iota_v$ for the group laws on points over arbitrary $R$-algebras; `hιt`, compatibility with the transition maps; `hιhecke`, existence for each generator $g$ of a compatible system of coalgebra endomorphisms inducing both $O.\mathrm{hecke}$ through $\iota$ and `genOpH` through $\Delta$; and `hιfin`, which states that the canonical morphism $j_v$ from the $p^v$-torsion of $O.G$ over $R$ is an open and a closed immersion whose image contains every point mapping to the closed point of $R$.
--
--   The Atkin–Lehner and degeneracy data. A semilinear automorphism $wgen$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` is given, together with `hwgen`, stating that $wgen$ computes the effect on places of the involution $\mathfrak{X}.w$ on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points; an algebra automorphism $\theta$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` with `hθ`, the condition that $\theta$ acts on elements coming from level $M/p$ by the $q$-expansion operator $q \mapsto q^p$, and `hwθ`, identifying $wgen$ with the semilinear automorphism of $\theta$. Two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra maps $\alpha_H, \beta_H$ from `xHFunctionFieldBar (M/p) (infSubgroup p M H hpM)` to `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` are given, each integral and satisfying the fundamental identity, finiteness and the pushforward norm formula (`hαHint`, `hαHFI`, `hαHfin`, `hαHN` and their $\beta$-analogues); `hdeg0` and `hdeg1` identify $O.\mathrm{degPts}\,0$ and $O.\mathrm{degPts}\,1$ with the divisor pushforwards along $\alpha_H$ and $\beta_H$. Additivity of $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ and of $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ is assumed (`hΛpts_add`, `hΛptsSp_add`). Additive endomorphisms $F, Finv, Fstar$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the special fibre are given with $F$ the $q$-expansion Frobenius pushforward mod $p$ (`hF`), $Finv$ a two-sided inverse of $F$ (`hFinv`) and $Fstar = p \cdot Finv$ (`hFstar`); a unit $pb$ of $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ reducing to $p$ (`hpb`) and the diamond operator $\delta$ attached to it (`hδ`). Finally, two degeneracy maps $\alpha_{\mathrm{pull}} : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to (J_H(M/p) \to J_H(M))$ and two morphisms $\mathrm{degPull}$ over the bases are given, compatible with the points (`hpull`), with the group laws (`hpull_mul`), with the special fibre in the form $(\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}^{-1}, Fstar)$ for $i = 0$ and $(Fstar, \delta)$ for $i = 1$ (`hpullsp`), and computed on divisor classes by pullback along $\alpha_H$ and $\beta_H$ respectively (`hpullα`, `hpullβ`).
--
--   Conclusion. For every $x \in J_H(M)$ lying in the image under the first Tate-module projection [`TateModule.proj p _ 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122) of the corner submodule $e_{i_0} \cdot T_p J_H(M)$, and lying in $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$, the following two assertions are equivalent:
--
--   (i) there exists a level-one point $y$ of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\Delta(y) = x$ which reduces to the identity, that is, such that for every $a \in \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,1$ the $Pl$-valuation of $\mathrm{toAlgHom}(y)(a) - \varepsilon(a)$, where $\varepsilon$ is the counit composed with the structure map $R \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, is $< 1$;
--
--   (ii) inertia acts cyclotomically on $x$: for every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and every natural number $c$ such that $\sigma \zeta = \zeta^c$ for all $p$-th roots of unity $\zeta$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, one has $\sigma \cdot x = c \cdot x$.
--
--   This is the local statement that the ordinary corner of $J_H(M)[p]$ carries no local-local part: on the finite part of the $p$-torsion, points of the corner cut out by an idempotent away from $U_p$ reduce to the identity in the $p$-divisible group over the inertia ring exactly when inertia at $p$ acts through the mod-$p$ cyclotomic character. The implication from cyclotomic action to reduction comes from `reducesToOne_of_inertia_cyclotomic_of_mem_finPts`, and the reverse implication is obtained by a counting argument comparing the cardinality of the corner with the number of inertia-cyclotomic points; it feeds the construction of the homomorphism from the $p$-torsion to polar differentials via $\mathrm{dlog}$ used in the level-lowering step at $p$.
